<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raghupati Raghava Raja Ram</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">origin not known; popularised by Gandhi, sung on the Salt March of <a href="/years/1930">1930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as रघुपति राघव राजा राम</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Raghupati Raghava Raja Ram&quot;, also called Ram Dhun, is a bhajan, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> devotional song, in praise of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>. Its origins are not clear. It is believed to have been written by the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a> or drawn from his Ramcharitmanas, or to come from a 17th-century sung prayer by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> saint-poet Ramdas.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The bhajan was set to music by Vishnu Digambar Paluskar in Raga Mishra Gara. Besides the attributions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a> and Ramdas, it is also claimed to come from the Nama Ramayana, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> devotional song attributed to Lakshmanacharya, which tells the Ramayana in 108 verses chanting the many names of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, each line ending with Rama. Only some versions of that work contain the verse Raghupati Raghava Raja Ram; others, such as the rendition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/m-s-subbulakshmi">M. S. Subbulakshmi</a>, do not.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The legend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> preferred, as Anthony Parel tells it in Gandhi's Philosophy and the Quest for Harmony, credits the song to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a>: on a pilgrimage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> temple of Dakore the poet refused to bow in prayer until Vishnu revealed himself as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, and Rama then appeared in his mind with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> and three devotees. Gandhi sang a version of his own, adding that the Ishwar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> of the Muslims are one, to spread a message of reconciliation between the two communities. After his return from Noakhali he replaced the refrain Bhaj man pyare Sitaram with Bhaj man pyare Rama Rahim, bhaj man pyare Krishna Karim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s changes drew objections from both sides. Some Muslims resented his placing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> on a par with Ram, and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> objected to Islamic elements in a Hindu bhajan; Srila Prabhupada set the version aside as one made to unite Hindus and Muslims rather than for pure devotion. The disputes continued. In September 2022 Mehbooba Mufti accused the BJP of pushing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> agenda after a government directive had schoolchildren in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> recite the song for Gandhi's 153rd birth anniversary, and in December 2024 the line Ishwar Allah tero naam set off protests at a BJP event in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>.</p><p>The chant appears in many films, among them Bharat Milap (1942), Jagriti (1954), Purab Aur Paschim (1970), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> (1982) and Lage Raho Munna Bhai (2006), where it is an important motif. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a> recorded it on his album Strangers and Cousins (1964), and a version by Ananda Shankar was played on a radio station of the game Grand Theft Auto: Liberty City Stories.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raghupati Raghava Raja Ram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
